WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.694
DNA strands run in opposite directions. This is called antiparallel. Notice that the

00:00:04.694 --> 00:00:09.389
template runs from three prime to five prime, while the complementary strand runs

00:00:09.389 --> 00:00:14.083
from five prime to three prime.

00:00:14.083 --> 00:00:19.028
New DNA grows from five prime to three prime. Each new nucleotide joins

00:00:19.028 --> 00:00:23.972
the three prime end of the growing complementary strand. The labels describe the

00:00:23.972 --> 00:00:28.917
strand ends, not two possible directions of synthesis.

00:00:28.917 --> 00:00:33.944
DNA polymerase cannot start a complementary strand from scratch. Primase makes a short

00:00:33.944 --> 00:00:38.972
RNA primer on the template. DNA polymerase can then extend the primer by

00:00:38.972 --> 00:00:44.000
adding DNA nucleotides at its three prime end.
